  "live_records": {
    "q-14376": {
      "dom_id": "step-q14376",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The point $P(-2,5)$ is reflected across the $x$-axis, shifted $3$ units down, and then reflected across the line $y=x.$ What is the resulting point?",
      "worked_solution": "Let's recall the functional representations of our three transformations:\n\n- A reflection in the $x$-axis can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,-y).\n$$\n\n- A shift of $3$ units down can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(x,y-3).\n$$\n\n- A reflection in the line $y=x$ can be represented by the function \n\n$$\n(x,y)↦(y,x).\n$$\n\nLet's apply the given transformations to the point $P$ using our functions:\n\nFirst, we apply the reflection. The image of the point $(-2,5)$ under the reflection is\n\n$$\n(-2,5)↦(-2,-5).\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we apply the shift. The image of the point $(-2,-5)$ under this shift is\n\n$$\n(-2,-5)↦(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nFinally, we apply the reflection in the line $y=x.$ The image of the point $(-2,-8)$ under this reflection is\n\n$$\n(-2,-8)↦(-8,-2).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resulting point is $(-8,-2).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
